        glyph = glyph_array[n_glyphs - 1].glyph;
        metrics = vertical ? &glyph->v_metrics : &glyph->h_metrics;
        if (vertical) {
            if (pen.y + metrics->advance_rotated.y > max_y) {
                max_y = pen.y + metrics->advance_rotated.y;
            }
        }
        else {
            if (pen.x + metrics->advance_rotated.x > max_x) {
                max_x = pen.x + metrics->advance_rotated.x;
            }
        }
    }
```

Wait, let me trace pen.x. After the loop, `pen.x` already includes the last advance. Let's verify: At the end of each iteration, `pen.x += metrics->advance_rotated.x;`. So after processing the last glyph, pen.x = (pen.x before last glyph) + advance_rotated.x of last glyph. For a trailing space, the advance is the space's advance. So pen.x IS the position after the space. Therefore:

```c
    if (vertical) {
        if (pen.y > max_y) max_y = pen.y;
    } else {
        if (pen.x > max_x) max_x = pen.x;
    }
```

This is exactly what `fill_text_bounding_box` does in the `pad` case. So for a trailing space, we want to apply the same extension even without pad.

But wait, should we handle rotation? For rotated text, the glyph positions are in rotated space. The pen.x/pen.y after the loop would include the rotated advances. `max_x` is computed in rotated coordinates, so extending by pen.x (rotated) is consistent.

Actually, let me think about the `vertical` case with trailing space. In vertical mode, `pen.y` accumulates. The vertical boundary uses `pen.y + bearing + glyph_height`. For a trailing space with zero height, max_y won't include the advance. So extending max_y with pen.y makes sense.

Now, but is the trailing space always last in array? In `size_text`, all chars that have glyph ids are included; even a space has a glyph id (space glyph exists in most fonts). So yes.

What about the case where a trailing space exists but with kerning/rotation? The pen.x already handles that.

One concern: What about `pad` already handling this? With pad, `pen.x > max_x` already extends. So with pad, adding our extension wouldn't change anything (max_x would already be pen.x). So the fix only affects non-pad mode, which matches the PR (boundary calculations without padding).

Now let me consider the char type. `PGFT_char` is `FT_UInt32`. In size_text, `ch` is `FT_UInt32`. `UNICODE_SPACE` is `((PGFT_char)' ')`. So comparing `slot->ch == UNICODE_SPACE` works.

